ver the weekend  and that's just on the basis of data  that's actually out since then  so things aren't quite as bad as that  but that's not to say they're good but  what  is frustrating and i've said it a few  times and i'll probably say again before  the end  is the fact that it's not easy to get a  hold of these numbers they're not being  provided by the government as a matter  of course  they're not telling us what their  reasonable worst case scenario is they  don't tell us what the story is they  just say oh we've broken it  they need to provide us with what the  actual scenario is and happily  now i think for the first time they  provide us with a bit more  uh data or at least a few more charts  they haven't made it easy for us to get  a hold of them i've had to trace some of  these ones here  um but for instance over the weekend  what we got was a projection of what  they expected to happen to deaths  so we'll keep an eye on that over the  next few weeks and work out whether we  are  really in line with where they think  we're heading of course  at some point the lockdown measures will  have an impact and will bear down  on this growth potentially but at least  for the next  two weeks or so before because it takes  about two weeks for the measures to kind  of be  seen on infection rates at least for the  next two weeks we'll get a sense of  whether that's  the right line or whether it's kind of  over um overstating it and the same  again for hospitalizations actually  we've had hospitalization data in uh  just the last day or two which look a  little bit better  than expected so again might it be the  case that this is  an overstatement and that we're not  necessarily going to hit that first  wave peak we will have to see but as i  say  it is disappointing let's say to put it  lightly at this stage in the pandemic a  pandemic that is all about data  that we're still having to extract this  stuff out of the government and  reverse engineer it and you know guess  basically because a lot of what i've  done has been guesswork i should say  at what they are trying to model inside  downing street  we need to know what the worst case  scenario is so we know whether we're  going to miss it or not  as it is all we know is that they say  that we've missed it and then  we can't even work out we're not even  told what the actual worst case scenario  is for those of us who follow data is  incredibly frustrating  but for everyone else it's frustrating  as well because what matters with a  pandemic is understanding what's  happening with the spread  and there is a lot of data out there  in the government's coffers which is not  releasing  and this is my final point really um  last  last week i don't know if you saw this  um i had the story about how the number  of care home cases in leeds and this is  what the line here  is number of care home cases and leads  has shot up and this basically shows you  that's the spring  rise in total covert cases and care  homes and leads and it goes down and  then it shoots up very sharply  um in the last week and a half and the  frustrating thing is  this data exists for pretty much every  area  of england and i presume it does for  scotland and wales too  and northern ireland as well but it's  not publicly released  it's not publicly released we wouldn't  know about the fact that there's a  sudden sharp uptick in care home cases  which is  deeply concerning given that that was  part of the problem that happened last  time  we wouldn't know about this had we at  sky news not managed to get a hold of it  this stuff should be released as a  matter of course this is  such an important point for the uk  economy  and for the uk you know health system  and for all of our well-being  and yet we're not being given the full  information about the state of the  disease  and it's unclear why not in other  countries around the world  many other countries belgium hong kong  taiwan  france as well they produce much more  data much more transparently  about what is happening with covert 19  and the more  data is produced the better an idea we  can have of  what's happening with this disease that  matters enormously  and yet it's as if the government  doesn't want to produce this stuff and  the nhs doesn't want to produce this  stuff publicly  and that's disappointing because until  it is  clear what's happening with this disease  it's unlikely that people are actually  going to  have their faith in the government and  faith in the nhs and as a result they  may not necessarily  be as likely to follow those lock down  rules it's about generating  trust in what you're doing as a  government and by not releasing this  data  and by coming up with spurious  explanations about  worst case scenarios that have been  missed without actually telling us what  the worst case scenario is  that doesn't really engender much trust
 

